@page "/web/crawler"
@inject CloudPage CloudPage

<PageIntro Package="AngryMonkey.CloudBlazor.Web" Title="Crawler Detection">
    <code>CloudPage.IsCrawler</code> is resolved from the request's <code>User-Agent</code> on
    construction, so a page can serve a simplified response to bots with no middleware.
</PageIntro>

<section class="section">
    <h2>This request</h2>

    <div class="result-grid">
        <ResultRow Label="Detected as">
            @if (CloudPage.IsCrawler)
            {
                <span class="badge badge-off">crawler</span>
            }
            else
            {
                <span class="badge badge-on">human</span>
            }
        </ResultRow>
        <ResultRow Label="User-Agent">
            <code>@_userAgent</code>
        </ResultRow>
        <ResultRow Label="Known substrings">
            <code>@CloudWebConfig.CrawlersUserAgents.Count</code>
        </ResultRow>
    </div>

    <p>
        To see it flip, request this page with a bot user agent:
    </p>

    <pre><code>curl -A "Googlebot/2.1 (+http://www.google.com/bot.html)" http://localhost:5000/web/crawler</code></pre>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <h2>Usage</h2>

    <pre><code>@@inject CloudPage CloudPage

@@if (CloudPage.IsCrawler)
{
    &lt;ServerRenderedSummary /&gt;
}
else
{
    &lt;InteractiveExperience /&gt;
}</code></pre>

    <p>MVC controllers deriving from <code>CloudController</code> use the helper instead.</p>

    <pre><code>public IActionResult Index() => IsCrawler() ? View("IndexSimple") : View();</code></pre>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <h2>Matching</h2>

    <p>
        The user agent is lower-cased and tested against a list of lower-cased substrings.
        Normalizing both sides is what makes entries such as <code>Baiduspider</code> or
        <code>AhrefsBot</code> match at all — comparing a lower-cased agent against a
        mixed-case list silently never matches.
    </p>

    <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="api-table">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Category</th>
                    <th>Examples</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Search engines</td><td><code>googlebot</code>, <code>bingbot</code>, <code>baiduspider</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Generic patterns</td><td><code>bot</code>, <code>crawler</code>, <code>spider</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Command-line tools</td><td><code>wget</code>, <code>curl</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>SEO and auditing</td><td><code>ahrefsbot</code>, <code>blexbot</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <p>The check is also available directly, which is what the tests exercise.</p>

    <pre><code>bool isCrawler = CloudWebConfig.IsCrawler(userAgent);</code></pre>
</section>

@code {
    [CascadingParameter] private HttpContext? HttpContext { get; set; }

    private string _userAgent = "(none)";

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        CloudPage
            .SetTitle("Crawler Detection")
            .SetDescription("Detect search engine crawlers from the request user agent and adapt the response.");

        string? userAgent = HttpContext?.Request.Headers.UserAgent.ToString();

        if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(userAgent))
            _userAgent = userAgent;
    }
}
